Sweet peace to sit in that bright state she ought,<br>''Unbloody'', or untroubled.
{{right|''B. Jonson'', King James’s Coronation Entertainment.}}}}
'''2.''' Not shedding blood; not cruel.—'''3.''' Not accompanied with bloodshed.
{{smaller block|Many battails, and some of those not ''unbloodie.''
{{right|''Milton'', Hist. Eng., ii.}}}}
{{smaller|'''Unbloody sacrifice''', a sacrifice which does not involve the shedding of blood, such as the meat-offering (meal-offering) of the Old Testament; specifically, the eucharistic sacrifice.}}

'''unblotted''' (un-blot′ed), ''a.'' Not blotted, or not blotted out; not deleted; not erased.
{{smaller block|Spenser . . . seems to have been satisfied with his first ''unblotted'' thoughts. {{right|''I. D’Israeli'', Amen. of Lit., II. 126.}}}}

'''unblown<sup>1</sup>''' (un-blōn′), ''a.'' [< ''un-''<sup>1</sup> + ''blown''<sup>1</sup>.] '''1.''' Not blown, inflated, or otherwise affected by the wind.
{{smaller block|<poem>A fire ''unblown'' (shall) devour his race.</poem>
{{right|''Sandys'', Paraphrase of the Book of Job, p. 31.}}
<poem>Leisure . . .
Might race with ''unblown'' ample garments.</poem>
{{right|''S. Lanier'', Sunrise on the Marshes.}}}}
'''2.''' Not caused to sound, as a wind-instrument.
{{smaller block|<poem>The lances unlifted, the trumpets ''unblown.''</poem>
{{right|''Byron'', Destruction of Sennacherib.}}}}

'''unblown<sup>2</sup>''' (un-blōn′), ''a.'' [< ''un-''<sup>1</sup> + ''blown''<sup>2</sup>.] Not blown; not having the bud expanded; hence, not fully grown or developed.
{{smaller block|<poem>My tender babes!
My ''unblown'' flowers!</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', Rich. III., iv. 4. 10.}}}}

'''unblushing''' (un-blush′ing), ''a.'' Not blushing; hence, destitute of or not exhibiting shame; impudent; shameless: as, an ''unblushing'' assertion.
